<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geoffrey of Bruyères</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1275">1275</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geoffrey of Bruyères was Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> in the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and a nephew of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a>. Celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> as the finest knight of the land, he fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> in 1259, was captured, and later had his barony restored after a rebellion. He died without a male heir.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Geoffrey, called Geoffrey of Briel in recent scholarship, was the son of Hugh of Briel and Alice of Villehardouin, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin</a>, second Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. The family came from Briel-sur-Barse in Champagne. Hugh inherited the Barony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> around 1222 from his brother Renaud, and Geoffrey was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> soon afterwards. The barony was the third largest in the principality, with 22 knights' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, and kept watch over the mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skorta">Skorta</a> area. Geoffrey succeeded his father in early 1238.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> credits Geoffrey with building the Castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>, and its Aragonese version says he kept a school of chivalry there where sons of the Greek nobility were trained as knights. He married Isabella de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I de la Roche</a>, Great Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-euboeote-succession">War of the Euboeote Succession</a> of 1256 to 1258 he first led his uncle's army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a>, then joined his father-in-law against the prince, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karydi">Battle of Karydi</a> he was pardoned but held his lands as a personal grant.</p><p>Captured after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a>, he was released in 1261 to carry the terms of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> to the nobles of the principality, who accepted them at the so-called Parliament of Ladies at Nikli. Geoffrey handed over the ceded castles to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. From 1263 to 1265 he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> without leave, ostensibly on pilgrimage but in fact living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with the wife of his vassal John of Katavas; he lost the barony again and was again restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1270 Geoffrey and the Baron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a> brought 150 horsemen and 200 infantry to the prince's raids into Byzantine Laconia. When the truce broke in 1275, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> gave him 50 horse and 200 crossbowmen to guard the passes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skorta">Skorta</a>, but he died of dysentery late that year. Because he held the barony by grant, it passed only to direct descendants and was divided, half to his widow and half to the prince's domain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 1320. Alfred Duggan made him the hero of the 1962 novel Lord Geoffrey's Fancy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Geoffrey of Briel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hugh's son <b>Geoffrey of Bruyeres</b> built the castle of Karytaina on a crag above the Alpheios around 1254.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-karytaina">Barony of Karytaina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.49,22.04&amp;year=1265" aria-label="Karytaina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5101 815 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Geoffrey of Bruyères"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5551 1085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5551 1085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.49,22.04&amp;year=1265">What was here in 1265? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
